ppy\nHappy\nHappy\nHappy\nUnhappy\nHappy\nUnhappy\nHappy\nUnhappy\nUnhappy\nHappy\nUnhappy\nUnhappy\nHappy\nHappy\nHappy\nUnhappy\nHappy\nUnhappy\nHappy\nHappy\nHappy\nHappy\nHappy\nUnhappy\nUnhappy\nHappy\nHappy\nHappy\nHappy\nHappy\nHappy\nUnhappy\nHappy\nUnhappy\nUnhappy\nUnhappy\nHappy\nHappy\nUnhappy\nHappy\nUnhappy\nHappy\nHappy\nUnhappy\nUnhappy\nHappy\nHappy\nUnhappy\nUnhappy\nHappy\nUnhappy\nHappy\nHappy\nHappy\nUnhappy\nUnhappy\nHappy\nHappy\nHappy\nHappy\nHappy\nUnhappy\nHappy\nHappy\nHappy\nUnhappy\nHappy\nUnhappy\nHappy\nHappy\nUnhappy\nUnhappy\nHappy\nHappy\nHappy\nUnhappy\nHappy\nHappy\nHappy\nUnhappy\nHappy\nHappy\nUnhappy\nHappy\nHappy\nUnhappy\nHappy\nUnhappy\nUnhappy\nUnhappy\nHappy\nHappy\nUnhappy\nHappy\nHappy\nHappy\nHappy\nHappy\nHappy\nHappy\nHappy\nHappy\nHappy\nHappy\nHappy\nHappy\nHappy\nHappy\nHappy\nHappy\nHappy\nHappy\nUnhappy\nHappy\nUnhappy\nHappy\nHappy\nHappy\nUnhappy\nUnhappy\nHappy\nHappy\nHappy\nUnhappy\nHappy\nUnhappy\nHappy\nUnhappy\nHappy\nUnhappy\nHappy\nHappy\nUnhappy\nHappy\nHappy\nUnhappy\nHappy\nHappy\nHappy\nHappy\nUnhappy\nUnhappy\nUnhappy\nUnhappy\nHappy\nHappy\nUnhappy\nHappy\nHappy\nHappy\nUnhappy\nHappy\nHappy\nHappy"}], "time_limit_ms": 2000, "memory_limit_mb": 512, "is_consistency_verified": false}
{"problem_id": "TACO-GP-16", "title": "Given an undirected graph with V vertices. We say ", "ground_truth_tag": "graphs", "rating": 995, "statement": "Given an undirected graph with V vertices. We say two vertices u and v belong to a single province if there is a path from u to v or v to u. Your task is to find the number of provinces.\nNote:  A province is a group of directly or indirectly connected cities and no other cities outside of the group. \nExample 1:\nInput:\n[\n [1, 0, 1],\n [0, 1, 0],\n [1, 0, 1]\n]\nOutput:\n2\nExplanation:\nThe graph clearly has 2 Provinces [1,3] and [2]. As city 1 and city 3 has a path between them they belong to a single province. City 2 has no path to city 1 or city 3 hence it belongs to another province.\nExample 2:\nInput:\n[\n [1, 1],\n [1, 1]\n]\nOutput :\n1\nYour Task:  \nYou don't need to read input or print anything. Your task is to complete the function numProvinces() which takes an integer V and an adjacency matrix adj as input and returns the number of provinces. adj[i][j] = 1, if nodes i and j are connected and adj[i][j] = 0, if not connected.\nExpected Time Complexity: O(V^{2})\nExpected Auxiliary Space: O(V)\nConstraints:\n1 ≤ V ≤ 500", "input_spec": "Standard competitive programming input format.", "output_spec": "Standard competitive programming output format.", "teacher_rationale": "1. Understand the Problem: We are given an undirected graph represented as an adjacency matrix of size V x V. A 'province' is defined as a connected component of the graph. We need to find the total number of connected components.\n2. Graph Modeling: The vertices represent cities (0 to V-1), and an edge exists between vertex i and vertex j if adj[i][j] == 1. \n3. Algorithmic Approach: We can traverse the graph using either Depth First Search (DFS) or Breadth First Search (BFS), or use a Disjoint Set Union (DSU) data structure. A simple traversal method (DFS/BFS) works efficiently: we iterate through all vertices from 0 to V-1. If a vertex has not been visited yet, it means we have found a new province. We increment our province count and perform a DFS/BFS traversal from this vertex to visit all other vertices reachable from it, marking them as visited.\n4. Complexity: \n   - Time Complexity: Since the graph is represented as an adjacency matrix, visiting all neighbors for a vertex takes O(V) time. Doing this for all V vertices results in O(V^2) time complexity, which matches the expected time complexity.\n   - Space Complexity: We need a visited array/set of size V, and the recursion stack for DFS can go up to depth V in the worst case. Thus, auxiliary space is O(V).", "teacher_solution": "import sys\n\n# Increase recursion depth just in case V is large (up to 500)\nsys.setrecursionlimit(2000)\n\nclass Solution:\n    def numProvinces(self, adj, V):\n        visited = [False] * V\n        provinces = 0\n        \n        def dfs(node):\n            visited[node] = True\n            for neighbor in range(V):\n                if adj[node][neighbor] == 1 and not visited[neighbor]:\n                    dfs(neighbor)\n        \n        for i in range(V):\n            if not visited[i]:\n                provinces += 1\n                dfs(i)\n                \n        return provinces\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    # Example testing code\n    V = 3\n    adj = [\n        [1, 0, 1],\n        [0, 1, 0],\n        [1, 0, 1]\n    ]\n    sol = Solution()\n    print(sol.numProvinces(adj, V))", "teacher_stated_tag": "graphs", "sample_tests": [{"input_data": "[\n [1, 0, 1],\n [0, 1, 0],\n [1, 0, 1]\n]", "output_data": "2"}, {"input_data": "[\n [1, 1],\n [1, 1]\n]", "output_data": "1"}], "time_limit_ms": 2000, "memory_limit_mb": 512, "is_consistency_verified": false}
{"problem_id": "TACO-GR-09", "title": "Xenny was a teacher and he had N students. The N c", "ground_truth_tag": "graphs", "rating": 1152, "statement": "Xenny was a teacher and he had N students. The N children were sitting in a room. Each child was wearing a white T-shirt, with a unique number from the range 1 to N written on it. T-Shirts of pink and blue color were to be distributed among the students by Xenny. This made the students very happy.\n\nXenny felt that a random distribution of T-Shirts would be very uninteresting. So, he decided to keep an interesting condition:\n\nEvery student would get a T-Shirt that is of a different color than his/her friends. That is, if X and Y are friends and X has a Pink T-Shirt, then Y should compulsorily have a Blue T-Shirt, and vice-versa.\n\nAlso, Xenny had a belief that Boys should wear blue T-Shirts and Girls should wear pink T-Shirts. If a boy was given a pink T-Shirt or a girl was given a Blue T-Shirt, he called it an inversion.\n\nSo, Xenny wanted to distribute T-Shirts in the above-mentioned interesting manner and also wanted to minimize \"inversions\". Help him solve the task.\n\nNote: There are no disjoint groups of friends in the room. That is, 2 distinct groups with finite number of students do not exist, but exactly 1 group of students exists in the given situation.\n\nInput Format:\nFirst line contains 2 space-separated integers - N and M - number of students and number of friendships present respectively.\n\nSecond line consists of N space-separated characters, where i^th character denotes the gender of the i^th student. B: Boy, G: Girl.\n\nM lines follow. Each line consists of 2 space-separated integers, u and v, showing that u is a friend of v and vice-versa.\n\nOutput Format:\nIf Xenny could distribute the T-Shirts in the desired way, print the minimum number of inversions required.\nElse, print \"Not possible\".\n\nConstraints:\n1 ≤ N ≤ 10^5\n1 ≤ M ≤ 10^5\n1 ≤ u, v ≤ N\n\nColours of T-Shirt are represented by uppercase characters 'B' and 'G'\n\nSAMPLE INPUT\n3 2\nB G B\n1 2\n1 3\n\nSAMPLE OUTPUT\n1\n\nExplanation\n\nStudent 1 can be given a Blue T-Shirt. Hence, Student 2 and 3 would receive Pink T-Shirts.\n\nSince, Student 3 is a Boy and has received a Pink T-Shirt, number of inversions = 1.", "input_spec": "Standard competitive programming input format.", "output_spec": "Standard competitive programming output format.", "teacher_rationale": "Step-by-step reasoning explaining the graphs approach:\n1. Problem Modeling: We are given N students with genders ('B' or 'G') and M friendships. The students form a single connected component (as stated: 'no disjoint groups of friends'). We need to assign colors (Pink or Blue) to each student such that if two students are friends, they must have different colors.\n2. Bipartite Graph Verification & Coloring: The condition that connected friends must have different colors means the graph of friendships must be bipartite. We can use Breadth-First Search (BFS) or Depth-First Search (DFS) to check if the graph is bipartite and to find the two possible coloring assignments. Since the graph is connected, once we pick a color for the first node, all other nodes' colors are uniquely determined.\n3. Inversions Minimization: An 'inversion' occurs when a Boy gets Pink or a Girl gets Blue. For a connected component, there are only two valid coloring configurations:\n   - Configuration 1: Assign Color 0 (say, Blue) to the starting node, and alternate accordingly.\n   - Configuration 2: Assign Color 1 (say, Pink) to the starting node, and alternate accordingly.\n4. We can compute the number of inversions for both valid configurations and take the minimum. If the graph is not bipartite, it's impossible to satisfy the condition, and we output 'Not possible'.\n5. Complexity: Time complexity is O(N + M) for traversing the graph and checking/counting inversions. Space complexity is O(N + M) to store the adjacency list and visited/color arrays.", "teacher_solution": "import sys\n\n# Increase recursion depth just in case, though BFS is preferred\nsys.setrecursionlimit(200000)\n\ndef solve():\n    input = sys.stdin.read\n    data = input().split()\n    \n    if not data:\n        return\n\n    N = int(data[0])\n    M = int(data[1])\n    \n    genders = data[2:N+2]\n    \n    adj = [[] for _ in range(N + 1)]\n    \n    idx = N + 2\n    for _ in range(M):\n        u = int(data[idx])\n        v = int(data[idx+1])\n        adj[u].append(v)\n        adj[v].append(u)\n        idx += 2\n        \n    color = [-1] * (N + 1)\n    \n    # Since the graph is guaranteed to be a single connected component, \n    # we only need to start BFS/DFS from node 1.\n    \n    # We will check bipartiteness and collect components\n    def get_components_and_check():\n        # returns (is_bipartite, component_nodes)\n        comp = []\n        queue = [1]\n        color[1] = 0\n        comp.append(1)\n        head = 0\n        \n        while head < len(queue):\n            u = queue[head]\n            head += 1\n            for v in adj[u]:\n                if color[v] == -1:\n                    color[v] = 1 - color[u]\n                    queue.append(v)\n                    comp.append(v)\n                elif color[v] == color[u]:\n                    return False, []\n        return True, comp\n\n    is_bip, comp = get_components_and_check()\n    \n    if not is_bip:\n        print(\"Not possible\")\n        return\n\n    # There are two possible color assignments for the bipartite graph:\n    # Assignment A: color as determined by `color` array.\n    # Assignment B: inverted colors (1 - color).\n    \n    # T-Shirt color mapping: 0 -> Blue ('B'), 1 -> Pink ('P' or 'G')\n    # Wait, problem says: T-Shirts of pink ('G'/'P'?) and blue ('B') color.\n    # Let's check constraints: ", "teacher_stated_tag": "graphs", "sample_tests": [{"input_data": "91 1026\nB B B B B B B B B B B B B B B B B B B B B B B B B B B B B B B B B B B B B B B 